Forward-declare all plc-emitted struct typedefs in dependencies.plc.h, and
normalise C identifier casing to match ST's case-insensitive semantics.

plc emits each POU's C header as `typedef struct { ... } NAME;` (anonymous
struct with typedef alias) and orders emissions by source order in the .st
file rather than by dependency. When one struct references a later-defined
one via a pointer -- e.g. `GEN_CRC16_type` in mGENx.h has field
`GEN_CRC16_TABLE_type* CRC16_TABLE;` while `GEN_CRC16_TABLE_type` is defined
further down -- the C compile fails with "unknown type name". Same hazard
applies across modules (mCOPx.h references CAN_CH_INIT_type from mCANx.h,
etc.).

Separately, ST identifiers are case-insensitive but plc's C emitter
preserves whatever surface spelling appears in each occurrence. When a .st
has `TYPE ETH_X_IFMIB_T` (definition, uppercase) and later
`ETH_X_IfMib_t: ETH_X_IfMib_T` (reference, mixed case), plc emits
`typedef struct ETH_X_IFMIB_T { ... } ETH_X_IFMIB_T;` for the definition
and `ETH_X_IfMib_T*` for the reference -- two distinct C identifiers for
what ST considers the same type.

Fix in three coordinated passes, all driven by generate_headers.py:

1. After `plc --generate-headers`, rewrite each emitted `<name>.h` to give
   every anonymous struct typedef a matching struct tag:
       typedef struct { ... } NAME;
     becomes
       typedef struct NAME { ... } NAME;
   Collect every NAME along the way; this is the canonical set of types.

2. Build a case-insensitive lookup {lowercase_name: canonical_name} and
   walk each header again. Inside every `typedef struct NAME { ... } NAME;`
   block, rewrite the FIRST identifier on each field-declaration line to
   its canonical spelling, if a canonical exists. Field names (the second
   identifier on the line) are left untouched, so a struct field like
   `ETH_X_IfMib_T* ETH_X_IfMib_t;` becomes `ETH_X_IFMIB_T* ETH_X_IfMib_t;`
   -- the type reference is normalised, the field name is preserved.

3. Render all canonical names into dependencies.plc.h as forward typedef
   declarations `typedef struct NAME NAME;`. Each generated header
   unconditionally #includes <dependencies.plc.h> as its first include,
   so any pointer to NAME is valid before NAME's struct body is seen.

C11 allows redundant typedef declarations that refer to the same type
(section 6.7 p3), so the forward `typedef struct NAME NAME;` and the later
`typedef struct NAME { ... } NAME;` in the same translation unit are both
well-formed.

The plc-emitted header itself has no unions or enums; only struct typedefs,
one per POU, without nesting. A simple non-greedy `\{[^{}]*\}` regex is
sufficient to locate each block.

